---
/**
 * /adult-drivers-ed/texas/new-resident-out-of-state-license — the reader who is EXEMPT.
 *
 * Rebuilt 2026-10-13 on the hook → answer → loop → funnel recipe. The reader holds a valid license from another
 * U.S. state, so the course does not apply to them and the page does not sell it: no `band`. The only bridge is
 * one sentence pointing a reader whose license expired more than two years ago to the expired-license page.
 */
import AdeGuide from '../../../components/ade/AdeGuide.astro';
import { law, newResident, transferIssuers, fees, itd, residency } from '../../../data/ade-eligibility';

const base = '/adult-drivers-ed/texas';
const url = `${base}/new-resident-out-of-state-license`;
const g = newResident.graceDays;
const issuers = `${transferIssuers.slice(0, -1).join(', ')} or ${transferIssuers.at(-1)}`;

// The one-breath answer: meta description and the first sentence of the lede.
const answer = `At 18 or older, a valid license from another U.S. state transfers to Texas with no drivers ed, knowledge test or driving test, per DPS.`;

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Do I need Texas drivers ed if I move here with an out-of-state license?', a: `No. §521.1601(b) says the driver education rule does not apply to a person who holds a valid driver license issued by another state. DPS goes further: if you are 18 or older and hold a valid out-of-state license, or one not expired over two years, you are exempt from the knowledge and skills exams as well as the ITD and adult driver education requirements.` },
  { q: 'How long do I have to switch to a Texas license?', a: `Up to ${g} days. You can drive on a valid, unexpired license from another U.S. state, U.S. territory, Canadian province or qualifying country for up to ${g} days after moving to Texas. After that you need the Texas license.` },
  { q: 'Do I have to give up my old license?', a: 'Yes. DPS requires you to surrender the out-of-state license when you transfer, and issues you the equivalent Texas version.' },
  { q: 'Do I need to register my car first?', a: 'Yes. DPS asks for evidence of Texas vehicle registration for each vehicle you own before you apply, and proof of vehicle registration applies specifically to new residents surrendering an out-of-state license.' },
  { q: 'What documents does a new Texas resident need for a driver license?', a: `Proof of identity, lawful presence, Texas residency and your Social Security number, plus Texas registration for each vehicle you own. Residency takes ${residency.documentsRequired} printed documents. ${residency.thirtyDayWaived}` },
  { q: 'Do I still need the ITAD video?', a: `Not if your transfer waives the skills test. DPS lists ITD among the requirements waived for an 18-and-over holder of a valid out-of-state license. If for any reason you do end up taking a driving skills test, the ${itd.adultName} certificate is required and lasts ${itd.certValidDays} days.` },
];

const next = [
  { href: `${base}/expired-out-of-state-license`, title: 'If the old license has expired', blurb: 'The two-year line DPS draws, and what changes on the far side of it.' },
  { href: `${base}/texas-driver-license-documents`, title: 'What to bring to the appointment', blurb: 'Identity, lawful presence, Social Security number and two printed proofs of residency.' },
  { href: `${base}/texas-driver-license-fees`, title: 'What the Texas license costs', blurb: 'Every DPS fee in one table, from a new license to a replacement.' },
  { href: `${base}/do-i-need-adult-drivers-ed`, title: 'Who does need the course', blurb: 'The Texas rule by age and by the license you already hold.' },
];
---

<AdeGuide
  title={`Moving to Texas With an Out-of-State License: No Drivers Ed, ${g} Days to Switch | Road Ready Safety`}
  description={answer}
  url={url}
  eyebrow="New residents"
  h1={`Moving to Texas with a valid out-of-state license? No course, no knowledge or driving test, ${g} days to switch`}
  lede={`${answer} You surrender the old card, pay $${fees.newLicense18to84}, pass the vision test, and you have ${g} days after moving to do it. Two things catch new residents out, and both are below: what DPS wants registered before you walk in, and how far the waiver stretches for a license that has already expired.`}
  faqs={faqs}
  dateModified="2026-10-13"
  updated="October 13, 2026"
  next={next}
  sources={[
    { label: 'Tex. Transp. Code §521.1601(b)', href: 'https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/TN/htm/TN.521.htm' },
    { label: 'Texas DPS, Moving to Texas: A Guide to Driver Licenses and IDs', href: 'https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/moving-texas' },
    { label: 'Texas DPS, Apply for a Texas Driver License', href: 'https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/apply-texas-driver-license' },
    { label: 'Texas DPS, Texas Residency Requirement for Driver Licenses and ID Cards', href: 'https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/texas-residency-requirement-driver-licenses-and-id-cards' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">Do you need Texas drivers ed if you move here with an out-of-state license?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      No, and DPS waives the knowledge and skills exams as well. It says so in one sentence on its Moving to Texas page:
    </p>
    <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-border pl-5 py-1 m-0">
      <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px] italic m-0">&ldquo;{law.dpsWaiverText}&rdquo;</p>
      <cite class="text-sm text-text-muted not-italic">&mdash; Texas DPS, Moving to Texas</cite>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      The waiver covers the driver education requirement even though §521.1601 would otherwise catch anyone
      under {law.ageCeiling}. It also reaches past a currently valid license: DPS extends it to a license
      <strong> not expired over two years</strong>, which is more generous than the statute reads on its face.
      A transfer with no knowledge or skills exam comes from {issuers}.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      If your old license expired more than two years ago, the waiver no longer applies, and{' '}
      <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href={`${base}/expired-out-of-state-license`}>the expired-license page</a>{' '}
      sets out what Texas asks of you instead.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">How long can you drive on your old license after moving to Texas?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Up to {g} days after you move. That is the window to register your vehicles, gather documents and get an
      appointment. Appointments are the slow part, so start at the beginning of the window rather than the end.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">What do you still have to do to transfer the license?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Four things, and the first one has to happen before the appointment.
    </p>
    <ul class="flex flex-col gap-3 pl-5 m-0 list-disc marker:text-text-subtle">
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><strong>Register your vehicles in Texas first.</strong> DPS asks for evidence of Texas registration for each vehicle you own, and singles out new residents surrendering an out-of-state license.</li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><strong>Surrender the old license.</strong> You are issued the equivalent Texas version.</li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><strong>Bring proof of identity, lawful presence, Texas residency and your Social Security number.</strong></li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><strong>Pay the ${fees.newLicense18to84} fee</strong> and take the vision test.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">What if your license is from another country?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Then the answer is different, and much less forgiving. The exemption is written for another
      <em> state</em>, and Texas holds reciprocity with only five countries. We set that out in{' '}
      <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href={`${base}/foreign-license-texas`}>moving to Texas with a license from another country</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</AdeGuide>
